# JUnit lifecycle, assertions, parameterized tests, and exceptions

## A JUnit test expresses a contract
JUnit examples require a test dependency in an external Maven or Gradle project; they are not directly available in the standard-library runner.
```java
@org.junit.jupiter.api.Test
void rejectsNegativeWidth() {
    org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows(
        IllegalArgumentException.class,
        () -> rectangleArea(-1, 4));
}
```
Arrange inputs, act on one behavior, then assert its observable result. assertThrows returns the exception so a stable code or safe message can be examined. Do not couple tests to every incidental wording choice. AssertJ is a popular fluent assertion library that reads as `assertThat(area).isEqualTo(12)` instead of raw JUnit calls, and it can be added alongside JUnit's runner when its chained, readable failure messages are worth the extra dependency.

@BeforeEach establishes per-test fixtures; @AfterEach releases resources. Parameterized tests use a source such as CsvSource or MethodSource to run a behavior across a readable case table. Keep test names and arguments sufficient to identify a failing row.

## Practice
Write parameterized area tests and a separate overflow test. Avoid catching exceptions manually and then forgetting to fail if no exception occurred. Add a deliberately broken implementation and confirm the tests fail for the intended reason before restoring it.

